strategy:
A single code node first checks infeasibility exactly as specified: unknown predecessors, cycles, and jobs with no fitting mode. It then builds calendar-aware capacity arrays. Initial solutions come from three sources:
- static priority rules;
- a new dynamic ATC/Giffler–Thompson dispatcher that ranks eligible jobs by their real slack on the partial schedule, swept over several lookahead and makespan-weight settings;
- biased random sampling.

change:
DIAGNOSIS: Weighted tardiness dominates the cost on the weak instances. In the worst cases tardiness is 10 to 50 times the makespan. Every starting solution comes from a static priority list fed to a serial SGS. Static rules cannot react to the partial schedule. They do not see that a bottleneck window (for example crew capacity 1 until t=189) has pushed every job late, or which jobs can still meet their due date. As a result they keep spending scarce early capacity on jobs with no due date. The SA cannot repair this on big instances, because one decode of 150 jobs costs about 1 ms. That leaves only a few hundred moves in the budget, so the search keeps the poor structure of its starting solutions. I add a new construction phase. It is a state-dependent dispatcher in the style of Giffler–Thompson combined with ATC. At each step it:
- computes, for every eligible job, the earliest feasible start in its best mode on the current resource profile;
- restricts the choice to jobs that can start before the earliest possible completion, which avoids needless idling;
- picks the job with the highest index. The index combines descendant tardiness weight, the actual slack against the due-propagated latest finish, and a makespan term based on the critical-path tail.

